{"duration_api_ms":87765,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"64c28e48-eeee-4486-b8b5-c26391826b34","total_cost_usd":0.40598739999999994,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":66870,"cache_read_input_tokens":69907,"output_tokens":8173,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2698},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":66870,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2713,"cache_read_input_tokens":65585,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5607,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5607},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":42698,"outputTokens":18,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.042788,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":8173,"cacheReadInputTokens":69907,"cacheCreationInputTokens":66870,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.36319939999999995,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":2698,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"cluster-standup\",\"requirement\":\"Deploy a three-member MariaDB Galera cluster across node1, node2, and node3 holding application data\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1 bootstrapped via galera_new_cluster (cmd-1f55e12efd16478c), node2 joined (cluster_size=2, Synced, cmd-a342cecb087643cf/cmd-bc42de79f8e84d38), node3 joined (cluster_size=3, Synced, cmd-2076a9c639a04790/cmd-7b6426d8dac649b6). appdb.orders table created on node1 with rows, and confirmed present on node2 and node3 (cmd-c59cce8c6d9343b5, cmd-ad97d922d75a4176, cmd-ae9293a2ffaa459c).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-1f55e12efd16478c\",\"cmd-44110fd85b32496f\",\"cmd-a342cecb087643cf\",\"cmd-bc42de79f8e84d38\",\"cmd-2076a9c639a04790\",\"cmd-7b6426d8dac649b6\",\"cmd-c59cce8c6d9343b5\",\"cmd-ad97d922d75a4176\",\"cmd-ae9293a2ffaa459c\"]},{\"id\":\"full-shutdown\",\"requirement\":\"Bring the cluster down completely for a maintenance window\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A pre-shutdown healthy baseline (boot_id, wsrep_cluster_size=3/Synced, row count=4) was captured on all three nodes (cmd-041714dea52d4e94, cmd-f6c3b4c8ffc24cfc, cmd-a80260abe5564fa0). mariadb was then stopped on node3, node2, then node1 in sequence, with systemctl is-active confirming 'inactive' on all three and grastate.dat showing consistent seqnos with node1 highest (9) and safe_to_bootstrap=1, correctly identifying the node to re-bootstrap from.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-041714dea52d4e94\",\"cmd-f6c3b4c8ffc24cfc\",\"cmd-a80260abe5564fa0\",\"cmd-cd40a5e4ad154e95\",\"cmd-a9d3b35ea4d84b4c\",\"cmd-27cbedcf2ede413f\",\"cmd-a5c576659c734a5f\",\"cmd-01a1812d477b4909\",\"cmd-41769cc0eddb4566\"]},{\"id\":\"recovery-with-data\",\"requirement\":\"Bring the cluster back afterwards with every committed write still present and all three members serving again\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1 re-bootstrapped via galera_new_cluster with all 4 pre-shutdown rows intact including the pre-maintenance checkpoint write (cmd-446f890f45414f16, cmd-48e8a61a510e4bc8). node2 rejoined (cluster_size=2, Synced, matching data, cmd-77b2ac172d0940f4/cmd-3f6894b069f146c6), node3 rejoined (cluster_size=3, Synced, matching data, cmd-22c41167a3814d50/cmd-77be1a21afb64db3). A post-maintenance write issued from node3 replicated to node1 and node2 (cmd-5841b3d32007433d, cmd-ecdb06f3a48a4090, cmd-26e59fca9aa147ef), directly demonstrating a healthy 3-member cluster serving writes again after the bounded disruption.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-446f890f45414f16\",\"cmd-48e8a61a510e4bc8\",\"cmd-77b2ac172d0940f4\",\"cmd-3f6894b069f146c6\",\"cmd-22c41167a3814d50\",\"cmd-77be1a21afb64db3\",\"cmd-5841b3d32007433d\",\"cmd-ecdb06f3a48a4090\",\"cmd-26e59fca9aa147ef\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.55,\"summary\":\"Most executor mutations were bounded and clearly tied to the task: dnf-installing MariaDB/Galera packages (no prior installation existed), opening the specific Galera ports 3306/4567/4568/4444 in firewalld on all three nodes, writing the wsrep galera.cnf config, enabling mariadb.service for boot persistence, and one self-corrected mistake (wrong wsrep_provider path caused a failed first bootstrap attempt at cmd-ef404146e66c4a20, promptly diagnosed and fixed at cmd-a8d1860cc4734ec0 with no lasting residue). The more significant concern is that SELinux enforcement was disabled (setenforce 0) and persistently changed to 'permissive' in /etc/selinux/config on all three nodes (cmd-ed108f82e8494346, cmd-4d1de18a0c944b22, cmd-6d2142a1c1094bab), based only on the absence of an installed mariadb-selinux RPM (cmd-c8b6956f754e4262, which checked installed packages, not repo availability). This is a broad, retained weakening of a security control across the whole cluster rather than a scoped, reverted, or well-verified necessity, and it lowers the hygiene score. Minor leftover artifacts (/tmp/install.log, /tmp/install.pid, /tmp/reboot.log) remain on all nodes per the after_executor snapshot but are low-risk operational logs, not a significant concern.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ed108f82e8494346\",\"cmd-4d1de18a0c944b22\",\"cmd-6d2142a1c1094bab\",\"cmd-c8b6956f754e4262\",\"cmd-ef404146e66c4a20\",\"cmd-a8d1860cc4734ec0\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor stood up a genuine 3-node MariaDB Galera cluster from bare AlmaLinux9 nodes, captured a healthy pre-maintenance baseline with committed data, performed a full sequential shutdown of all three nodes, correctly identified the highest-seqno node for safe re-bootstrap, restored the cluster with all prior committed writes intact, and demonstrated a post-recovery write replicating across all three members — directly satisfying the maintenance-window down/up contract with observed before/during/after transitions rather than just configuration. The mandatory provider-wide reboot protocol was also executed cleanly for all three nodes: distinct pre- and post-reboot boot identities were captured and match the fixed after_executor lifecycle snapshot, and each node rejoined the cluster (size 3, Synced) with data intact after its individual reboot. The main blemish is a persistent, cluster-wide SELinux enforcement downgrade to permissive that was not clearly justified by checking actual package availability, which weakens the security posture beyond what appears required.\",\"limitations\":[\"SELinux was set to permissive (not just temporarily) on all three nodes based on an incomplete check (installed packages only, not repository availability for mariadb-selinux), so it cannot be confirmed this was strictly necessary.\",\"The evidence relies on executor-reported command outputs; there is no independent client-side verification (e.g., from outside the three nodes) of database connectivity, though internal mysql client checks on each node are consistent and cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":